In the limit of zero viscosity (







 \eta \to 0 







), the fluid becomes an ideal fluid, and the viscous stresses vanish, leaving only the pressure term 







 \sigma_{ij} = -P \delta_{ij} 







. In the limit of an incompressible fluid (







 \nabla \cdot \mathbf{v} = 0 







), the last term in the viscous stress tensor becomes zero, simplifying it to 







 \sigma_{ij} = -P \delta_{ij} + \eta (\partial_j v_i + \partial_i v_j) 







. As the fluid velocity gradients approach zero (







 \partial_j v_i \to 0 







), the viscous stresses also tend to zero, and the stress is dominated by pressure.
